mhartid@ harea harr @ mscr! ;

\ ksleep  the calling thread, in a call, sleeps: the hart goes back to its
\ loop (as when the thread stops), and when trun runs the thread again it
\ goes on here, the call's stacks as they were.  Whoever calls it has made
\ the thread wait (state 4, kind 7, its token at 192) and woken it on
\ what it waits for; the on-a-hart flag keeps it from running elsewhere
\ until this hart is off it.  Only from a call (kthr?).
: ksleep
F3220034 ,   \ csrrs t0,mscratch,zero
23A22210 ,   \ sw sp,0x104(t0)
23A48211 ,   \ sw s8,0x108(t0)
23A6B211 ,   \ sw s11,0x10C(t0)
23A83210 ,   \ sw gp,0x110(t0)
23A08212 ,   \ sw s0,288(t0)
23A29212 ,   \ sw s1,292(t0)
23A42213 ,   \ sw s2,296(t0)
23A63213 ,   \ sw s3,300(t0)
23A84213 ,   \ sw s4,304(t0)
23AA5213 ,   \ sw s5,308(t0)
23AC6213 ,   \ sw s6,312(t0)
23AE7213 ,   \ sw s7,316(t0)
73231034 ,   \ csrrs t1,mepc,zero
23AE6210 ,   \ sw t1,0x11C(t0)
17030000 ,   \ L1: auipc t1,0x0
13030303 ,   \ addi t1,t1,48
23AA6210 ,   \ sw t1,0x114(t0)
13031000 ,   \ addi t1,zero,1
23AC6210 ,   \ sw t1,0x118(t0)
03A10208 ,   \ lw sp,128(t0)
03AC4208 ,   \ lw s8,132(t0)
03AD8208 ,   \ lw s10,136(t0)
83ADC208 ,   \ lw s11,140(t0)
83A10209 ,   \ lw gp,144(t0)
03A34209 ,   \ lw t1,148(t0)
67000300 ,   \ jalr zero,0(t1)
;

\ kthr? ( -- flag )  the kernel runs a thread's call (not its own work).
: kthr?  area@ mhartid@ harea harr @ <> ;

\ kwaiton ( token -- )  under klk: the calling thread sleeps until token is
\ woken (fwake); klk is given up first.  It returns without klk, and the
\ caller looks again at what it waited for (a wake may be for another).
: kwaiton  area@ C0 lit + !  7 lit area@ D4 lit + !  4 lit area@ tstate!  klk @ unlock ksleep ;

\ kmlock ( m -- ), kmunlock ( m -- )  a kernel mutex, the word at m (0
\ free): a thread waiting for it sleeps, so it may be held across a sleep
\ (unlike klk, held only briefly).  Outside a thread's call (at boot, in
\ the store's benchmark) it is waited for by spinning.
: kmlock
begin
	klk @ lock
	dup @ 0= if 1 lit over ! klk @ unlock 1 lit else
	kthr? if dup kwaiton else klk @ unlock then 0 lit then
until drop ;
: kmunlock  klk @ lock 0 lit over ! fwake klk @ unlock ;
: kmtry  klk @ lock dup @ 0= if 1 lit swap ! 1 lit else drop 0 lit then klk @ unlock ;   \ ( m -- ok )  taken if free

\ tafter ( t -- )  a thread back from running: gone if its process is
\ ending, ready again if its slice ran out (or it yielded).
: tafter
klk @ lock
0 lit over DC lit + !
dup tstate 7 lit = if 0 lit swap tstate! else
dup tproc 28 lit + @ if
	dup tstate dup 0= swap 6 lit = or over 118 lit + @ or if drop else tdead then
else
	dup tstate 2 lit = if 1 lit over tstate! then
	tstate 1 lit = if hpoke then
then then
klk @ unlock ;

: kready variable ;          \ the tables made: threads may be run


\ kspawn ( name tickets -- pid | error )  a process the kernel starts, its
\ 0, 1 and 2 the console.
\ ufd ( fd -- f | 0 )  the calling process's descriptor fd.
: ufd  dup 1F lit u> if drop 0 lit else area@ tproc pfd @ then ;

\ fdput ( f p -- fd | -24 )  a descriptor of p on f, the lowest free.
: fdput
FFFFFFE8 lit
20 lit 0 lit do
	dup 0< if over i swap pfd @ 0= if drop i then then
loop
dup 0< if nip nip else rot over 3 lit pick pfd ! nip then ;

\ pcons ( p -- )  its 0, 1 and 2 on a new console file.
: pcons
1 lit 3 lit fnew ?dup if
	3 lit over 4 lit + !
	3 lit 0 lit do dup 2 lit pick i swap pfd ! loop drop
then drop ;

\ A process's arguments are at 20 KiB into its memory (where its heap would
\ begin): their length, then the strings, each NUL-ended, an empty one
\ last; the first is the program's name.  lib/user.fam's start puts the
\ heap past them.

\ kargs ( name p -- )  the arguments of a process the kernel starts.
: kargs
10 lit + @ 5000 lit +
0 lit begin 2 lit pick over + c@ ?dup while 2 lit pick 4 lit + 2 lit pick + c! 1 lit + repeat
2 lit + 3 lit + FFFFFFFC lit and swap ! drop ;

: kspawn
klk @ lock over swap 0 lit pspawn
dup 0< 0= if dup 1 lit - prn dup pcons 2 lit pick swap kargs then nip
klk @ unlock ;
